---
import '../styles/global.css';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navigation from '../components/Navigation.astro';
import BackButton from '../components/BackButton.astro';
import CircuitBreaker from '../components/CircuitBreaker.tsx';
---

<BaseLayout title="Circuit Breaker" description="Instant calm when anxiety strikes. Grounding exercises, calming words, and gentle guidance to break the cycle.">
  <div class="page">
    <div class="page__header">
      <BackButton />
      <h1 class="page__title">Circuit Breaker</h1>
      <p class="page__desc">Instant calm when you need it</p>
    </div>
    <CircuitBreaker client:load />
  </div>

  <article class="article-guide">
    <div class="article-guide__inner">
      <h2>Breaking the Cycle of Late-Night Anxiety</h2>
      <p>
        Waking up in the middle of the night with a racing heart, tight chest, or overwhelming dread is one of the most frightening experiences a person can face. At 3:00 AM, the rational prefrontal cortex operates at a significantly diminished capacity, while the primitive emotional center (the amygdala) remains hyper-reactive. Without daytime distractions, minor worries magnify into catastrophic emergencies.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>Circuit Breaker</strong> protocol was created specifically to interrupt this neurological feedback loop. Rather than trying to debate or "solve" your fears in the dark, the objective is to physically ground your sensory perception back into the safe, immediate present moment.
      </p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <strong>The Nocturnal Cortisol Window:</strong> Your body naturally begins increasing cortisol production around 3:00 to 4:00 AM to prepare you for morning waking. If you are already carrying high baseline stress, this natural hormonal rise can trigger sudden nocturnal panic.
      </div>

      <h3>The 5-4-3-2-1 Grounding Method Explained</h3>
      <p>
        Sensory grounding is a cornerstone of dialectical behavior therapy (DBT) and trauma de-escalation. When panic floods the bloodstream with adrenaline, verbal reasoning fails. Grounding works by forcefully recruiting the primary sensory cortices:
      </p>
      <div class="guide-card-grid">
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>5 Things You Can See</h4>
          <p>Scan your dimly lit room for shapes, shadows, or textures. Observing neutral items diverts attention away from internal terror.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>4 Things You Can Feel</h4>
          <p>Notice the cool texture of the bedsheets, the firmness of the mattress, the weight of the blanket, or the softness of your pillow.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>3 Things You Can Hear</h4>
          <p>Listen closely for distant ambient sounds: a gentle hum of an appliance, the wind outside, or your own slow exhalations.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Emergency Action Protocol for Nocturnal Panic</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Acknowledge the surge:</strong> Remind yourself immediately: "I am having an adrenaline surge. My body is sounding a false alarm. I am completely safe in this room, and this feeling will peak and pass within minutes."</li>
        <li><strong>Lengthen your exhale:</strong> Take a normal breath in, but blow it out slowly through pursed lips, like cooling a hot spoonful of soup. Repeat this three to four times to prevent hyperventilation.</li>
        <li><strong>Drop your shoulders and unclench:</strong> Check your jaw, stomach, and pelvic floor. Consciously let go of the physical armor you have put up against the feeling.</li>
        <li><strong>The 20-Minute Rule:</strong> If high anxiety persists after 20 minutes in bed, do not lie there tossing and turning in agony. Gently get out of bed, move to a dimly lit room, read a physical book or listen to calming audio, and only return to bed when your eyelids feel heavy.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="guide-disclaimer">
        <strong>Wellness Notice:</strong> Circuit Breaker grounding is an emotional self-soothing and relaxation aid. It is not an emergency psychiatric intervention or a substitute for medical evaluation. If you experience severe chest pain, shortness of breath, numbness radiating to your arm, or feel at risk of harm, contact emergency services or your local crisis helpline immediately.
      </div>

      <h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why is anxiety so much worse at night?</summary>
          <p>At night, ambient silence and darkness remove the sensory inputs that normally keep our minds anchored during the day. Furthermore, natural hormonal fluctuations (such as early-morning cortisol surges) combine with reduced prefrontal cortex activity, making fears feel far more intense and urgent.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is waking up with a racing heart dangerous?</summary>
          <p>In the vast majority of healthy adults, nocturnal heart racing is caused by a benign adrenaline spike triggered by a nightmare, sleep position change, or brief oxygen fluctuation. However, if heart palpitations are accompanied by dizziness, chest pressure, or fainting, consult a physician promptly.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Should I stay in bed if I cannot calm down?</summary>
          <p>If you have been feeling intense anxiety or frustration for more than 20 to 30 minutes, sleep experts recommend getting out of bed. Staying in bed while panicked trains your brain to associate the mattress with stress rather than rest.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does the 5-4-3-2-1 technique help with panic?</summary>
          <p>The 5-4-3-2-1 technique forces the brain to process external sensory data (vision, touch, hearing). This shifts neural activity away from the alarm centers of the amygdala and re-engages the rational frontal lobes.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can using my phone in the middle of the night make anxiety worse?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Bright blue light suppresses melatonin, and scrolling social media or news feeds injects fresh emotional triggers into an already vulnerable brain. If you must use a screen, keep brightness at the absolute minimum and use dark-mode, single-purpose relaxation tools.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <Navigation slot="nav" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: 32rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1rem 0.5rem 6rem;
  }

  .page__header {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .page__title {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0.25rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text);
  }

  .page__desc {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
